sum='The child watches kites flying high in the windy sky; they look like coloured birds. The child wishes to be small and as light as air, so that it could climb onto a kite and sail up into the sky. It imagines drifting on the kite’s paper wings and hearing the songs of the wild wind. How much fun it would be to look right down over the park and the rooftops of the town! The people below would stand and stare and wish that they were the child, flying high, high in the air. The poet Daphne Lister, who grew up in northeast England, was a primary teacher and wrote joyful poems for children, often inspired by nature and the sea. Poetic devices: simile (like coloured birds; light as air), alliteration (wind-whipped; wild wind), repetition (high, high), rhyme (fly–sky, air–there, wings–sings, down–town, stare–air).',
